# Quiz Arena — Technical Overview

This is a standalone write-up for anyone taking over the project, auditing
it, or just trying to understand how it's put together — a new developer,
the CEO, whoever. It's not part of the app and isn't linked from it.
It describes the app **as it exists today**, including the fixes made in
the most recent round of work (white screen on login, recurring install
prompt, a Friend Groups bug, and the marketplace payment-confirmation
screen).

---

## 1. What this is

Quiz Arena is a **CBT (Computer-Based Test) practice platform** built for
UNIMED (a Nigerian university) 100-Level students. Students practice
timed, randomized quizzes for free, and can buy premium quizzes (paid via
Flutterwave) that behave like real exam-condition CBTs. There's also a
social layer — "Friend Groups" — where students team up, and a weekly
"Global Challenge" leaderboard with a cash prize.

There are two roles:

- **Student** — the main audience, everything described below.
- **Admin** — a single hardcoded account (`admin@test.com`, see
  `js/main.js`) that manages subjects, quizzes, questions, and sends
  notifications.

## 2. Tech stack

- **No framework, no build step.** This is plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript,
  loaded directly by the browser as native **ES modules**
  (`<script type="module" src="js/main.js">` in `index.html`). There's no
  webpack/vite/React — every `import`/`export` you see is the browser's
  native module system, and every file is served as-is.
- **Firebase** is the entire backend:
  - **Firebase Auth** — email/password + Google sign-in.
  - **Firestore** — the database (see §5).
  - **Firebase Cloud Functions** (`functions/index.js`) — the only
    trusted server-side code, used for payment verification and push
    notifications.
  - **Firebase Cloud Messaging** — push notifications.
  - **Firebase Hosting** (implied by `firebase.json` / `.firebaserc`) —
    where this is deployed.
- **Flutterwave** — payment provider for purchasing quizzes (Nigerian
  cards, USSD, bank transfer).
- **Cloudinary** (`js/cloudinary/config.js`) — image hosting, used by the
  admin panel for quiz/question images.
- It's a **PWA** (Progressive Web App) — installable to a phone home
  screen, works via a service worker (`sw.js`) and `manifest.json`.

There is no npm build for the frontend at all — the only `package.json`
in the repo lives under `functions/`, for the Cloud Functions.

## 3. How the app boots (and why the white-screen fix matters)

`index.html` is the single HTML page. It:

1. Paints an inline, dependency-free **boot loader** (spinner + logo)
   immediately — this is plain `<style>` in the `<head>`, not an external
   stylesheet, specifically so it renders before anything else has to
   load.
2. Loads all the page CSS files as `<link>` tags (see §7).
3. Loads `js/main.js` as a module — this is the actual entry point.

`js/main.js`:

1. Registers the service worker.
2. Calls Firebase's `onAuthStateChanged`, which tells us whether someone
   is already logged in (from a previous session) or not — this check
   itself takes a moment, and then if they're logged in, we make a
   Firestore call to fetch their user document. **That round-trip is
   what used to leave signed-in users staring at a blank white screen**
   — the boot loader now covers that entire window and is only removed
   once the real page (`renderLanding`, `renderStudentDashboard`, or
   `renderAdminDashboard`) has actually rendered into `<div id="app">`.
3. Routes to one of three renders based on auth state + role:
   - No user → `renderLanding()` (`js/ui/landing.js`) — the marketing
     page + login/register form.
   - `user.email === "admin@test.com"` → `renderAdminDashboard()`.
   - Any other logged-in user → fetch their Firestore doc, then
     `renderStudentDashboard(userData)`.

There's no router library — this is a hand-rolled single-page app. Every
"page" is a function that does `document.getElementById("app").innerHTML
= \`...\``and then wires up event listeners. Back/forward browser
navigation is handled manually via`history.pushState`+ a`popstate`listener in`js/student/navigation.js`.

## 4. Project structure

```
quizArena/
├── index.html              Single HTML page (see §3)
├── manifest.json            PWA manifest (icons, theme color, name)
├── sw.js                    Service worker (basic install/cache)
├── firebase-messaging-sw.js Service worker for push notifications
├── firebase.json / .firebaserc   Firebase Hosting + project config
│
├── js/
│   ├── main.js               Entry point — auth routing (§3)
│   ├── auth.js                Login/register/Google/reset/logout
│   ├── sessionManager.js      Auto-logout after 1hr of inactivity
│   ├── installPrompt.js       Shared PWA "install" event capture
│   ├── notificationsService.js  Writes to the `notifications` collection
│   ├── pushNotifications.js   FCM token registration
│   │
│   ├── firebase/config.js     Firebase project keys + SDK init
│   ├── cloudinary/config.js   Cloudinary upload config (admin images)
│   │
│   ├── ui/landing.js          Logged-out landing page + auth forms
│   │
│   ├── student/                Everything a logged-in student sees
│   │   ├── dashboard.js         Student home page
│   │   ├── practice.js          Free practice quizzes
│   │   ├── quiz.js               The actual quiz-taking screen (practice)
│   │   ├── purchasedQuiz.js      The actual quiz-taking screen (paid)
│   │   ├── questionNavigator.js  Question grid/jump-to-question UI
│   │   ├── reviewAnswers.js      Post-quiz answer review
│   │   ├── myQuizzes.js          List of quizzes the student owns
│   │   ├── marketplace.js        Browse + buy paid quizzes (§8)
│   │   ├── purchaseService.js    Firestore writes for purchases
│   │   ├── friendGroups.js       Friend Groups + leaderboard UI (§9)
│   │   ├── friendGroupService.js Firestore logic for groups/leaderboard
│   │   ├── attemptsService.js    Records every quiz attempt, streaks
│   │   ├── analyticsWidgets.js   Dashboard score-trend widgets
│   │   ├── installNudge.js       Recurring "install app" banner (§10)
│   │   ├── loadingOverlay.js     Reusable animated loading spinner
│   │   ├── navigation.js         Manual back-button handling
│   │   └── scrollEffects.js      Landing-page scroll reveal/parallax
│   │
│   └── admin/                  Admin-only panel
│       ├── dashboard.js         Admin home / stats
│       ├── subjects.js          CRUD for subjects
│       ├── quizzes.js           CRUD for paid quizzes
│       ├── questions.js         CRUD for quiz questions
│       ├── notifications.js     Send a push notification to students
│       ├── cascadeDelete.js     Deleting a subject also cleans up its quizzes/questions
│       └── dangerDialog.js      Shared "are you sure?" confirmation modal
│
├── css/
│   ├── base.css            Global reset + design tokens (colors, radius, shadows — see §7)
│   ├── auth.css             Landing/login/register page
│   ├── responsive.css       Mobile overrides — loaded LAST, see file header
│   ├── installNudge.css     The recurring install banner
│   ├── student/*.css        One file per student page, named to match
│   └── admin/*.css          One file per admin page, named to match
│
├── functions/
│   └── index.js             Cloud Functions (§6)
│
└── icons/, images/           Static assets
```

**Naming convention worth knowing:** almost every "page" has three
matching files spread across three folders —
`js/student/marketplace.js` + `css/student/marketplace.css` — that's the
pattern everywhere, so if you're looking for where something on screen
comes from, the JS and CSS filenames match.

## 5. Firestore data model

There's no formal schema definition (Firestore is schemaless) — this is
reconstructed from what the code actually reads/writes. Collections:

| Collection          | Written by                                                        | Shape                                                                                                                                                                                                     |
| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `users`             | `auth.js` on signup, admin panel                                  | `{ name, email, role: "student"\|"admin", purchasedQuizzes: [purchaseId...], fcmTokens: [...], createdAt }`                                                                                               |
| `subjects`          | `js/admin/subjects.js`                                            | Subject list shown in Practice/Marketplace filters                                                                                                                                                        |
| `quizzes`           | `js/admin/quizzes.js`                                             | Paid quiz metadata: title, price, subject, level, semester                                                                                                                                                |
| `questions`         | `js/admin/questions.js`                                           | Individual questions, linked to a quiz or subject                                                                                                                                                         |
| `attempts`          | `attemptsService.js` (called from `quiz.js` / `purchasedQuiz.js`) | `{ userId, mode: "practice"\|"purchased", subjectName, quizId, quizTitle, score, totalQuestions, percentage, completedAt }` — this is the record every score/streak/leaderboard calculation is built from |
| `purchases`         | **Cloud Function only** (`verifyFlutterwavePurchase`)             | `{ userId, quizId, status: "paid", purchasedAt }` — the source of truth for "does this student own this quiz"                                                                                             |
| `friendGroups`      | `friendGroupService.js`                                           | `{ name, code, ownerId, ownerName, memberIds: [uid...], createdAt }`                                                                                                                                      |
| `groupJoinRequests` | `friendGroupService.js`                                           | `{ groupId, requesterId, requesterEmail, requesterName, status: "pending"\|"accepted"\|"rejected" }`                                                                                                      |
| `notifications`     | `notificationsService.js`                                         | Triggers the `sendNotificationPush` Cloud Function (§6)                                                                                                                                                   |

**Important architectural pattern used throughout:** each collection is
meant to have exactly **one "service" module that's allowed to write to
it** (e.g. `purchaseService.js` for `purchases`, `friendGroupService.js`
for `friendGroups`/`groupJoinRequests`, `attemptsService.js` for
`attempts`). UI files call into the service, never touch Firestore
directly for that collection. This is enforced by convention/comments in
the code, not by tooling — worth keeping in mind if you add a new
feature.

## 6. Cloud Functions (`functions/index.js`)

Only two functions, and both exist because the browser cannot be
trusted with certain writes:

1. **`verifyFlutterwavePurchase`** (callable function) — the _only_ place
   a purchase is ever marked `"paid"`. The client calls this after
   Flutterwave's checkout widget reports success, but the function
   **independently re-verifies the transaction directly with
   Flutterwave's API** before writing to Firestore. This exists so a
   student can't fake a browser-side "payment successful" response and
   grant themselves a free quiz.
2. **`sendNotificationPush`** (Firestore trigger) — fires automatically
   whenever a document is created in `notifications`. Looks up FCM
   device tokens (either one student, or every student for a broadcast)
   and sends the push, then prunes any tokens that bounced (uninstalled
   app, revoked permission, etc.).

## 7. CSS approach

- `css/base.css` is the global reset + a set of CSS custom properties
  (`--color-primary`, `--radius-md`, `--shadow-md`, etc.) added in the
  latest pass so colors/spacing have named references instead of only
  scattered hex codes. Existing page CSS wasn't force-migrated to these
  variables (would've meant touching ~6,500 lines with real regression
  risk) — new components should prefer them going forward.
- Each page owns its own CSS file (see the folder structure in §4) —
  there's no shared component library, so similar-looking elements
  (buttons, cards) are sometimes redefined per-file rather than shared.
  That's a known area for future cleanup, not a bug.
- `css/responsive.css` is loaded **last**, after every page's own
  stylesheet, and only contains mobile-width overrides — the comment at
  the top of that file explains why the load order matters (CSS cascade:
  it needs to win at shared breakpoints).
- No CSS framework (no Tailwind/Bootstrap) — everything is handwritten.

## 8. Payment flow (Marketplace)

1. Student taps "Buy" on a quiz card → `openPurchaseDialog()` shows a
   confirmation dialog with price.
2. On confirm, `js/student/marketplace.js` launches Flutterwave's inline
   checkout widget (loaded via `<script src="https://checkout.flutterwave.com/v3.js">`
   in `index.html`).
3. Flutterwave's callback reports a status. Card payments resolve
   immediately (`successful`/`completed`); bank transfers often come
   back `pending` first and get **polled** (`pollForConfirmation`, every
   5s, up to 6 tries) rather than treated as failed.
4. Either way, the client never trusts Flutterwave's client-side status
   directly — it calls `confirmFlutterwavePurchase()`
   (`purchaseService.js`), which hits the `verifyFlutterwavePurchase`
   Cloud Function (§6) to re-verify server-side before anything is
   written.
5. **Once confirmed**, the dialog now shows an explicit green
   "Payment Successful!" screen naming the quiz, with a "Continue"
   button — added in the latest fix, because previously the dialog just
   silently closed and only the card underneath changed to "Owned",
   which read as "did this actually work?" on a slow connection. The
   marketplace grid re-renders (pulling a fresh Firestore read, not
   cached state) only once the student taps Continue.

## 9. Friend Groups & the Global Challenge

- A student can **own** at most 1 group and **belong to** at most 3
  total (`MAX_GROUPS_OWNED` / `MAX_GROUPS_JOINED` in
  `friendGroupService.js`).
- Joining requires the group code and goes through an
  accept/reject request flow — the group owner approves.
- A group only unlocks the **Global Challenge** (a weekly ₦1000 prize,
  reset every Monday) once it has 3+ members.
- The Challenge score isn't purely "highest average" — it blends: your
  own average score this week (65%), your attempt _volume_ this week up
  to a cap (20%), and your best qualifying group's average as a small
  nudge (15%). See `CHALLENGE_SCORE_WEIGHT` etc. in
  `friendGroupService.js`.
- **Bug fixed in the latest pass:** the leaderboard was meant to show
  how many _different_ quizzes/subjects a student attempted this week
  ("variety" — the code explicitly rewards trying different things over
  repeating one quiz), but the number was never actually calculated, so
  it displayed literally as `undefined quizzes`. This is now computed
  from distinct `quizId`/`subjectName` values per user, per week.

## 10. PWA install prompt

- `js/installPrompt.js` is the single place that listens for the
  browser's `beforeinstallprompt` event (it only fires once per session,
  so only one listener should ever claim it).
- `js/student/installNudge.js` shows a floating, dismissible banner on
  the dashboard. Dismissing it **snoozes it for 3 days** rather than
  hiding it forever — it'll come back on a later visit until the
  student actually installs, or the browser stops offering the prompt
  because it's already installed.
- The landing page's own "⬇️ Install App" button (`js/ui/landing.js`)
  now reads from this same shared module instead of keeping its own
  separate listener, so both surfaces stay in sync.

## 11. Known constraints / things a new developer should know

- **The admin account is hardcoded** (`admin@test.com`, in `main.js`)
  — there's no role-management UI to add a second admin. Anyone signing
  up with that exact email becomes admin.
- **No automated tests.** Nothing in this repo runs `npm test`. Changes
  are verified manually / via `node --check` for syntax.
- **No build step** means no bundling, minification, or tree-shaking —
  every file is served exactly as written, and every import is a real
  network request in production. This keeps the mental model simple but
  means page weight scales linearly with file count.
- **Firestore composite indexes**: a couple of queries (e.g. in
  `attemptsService.js`) combine `where()` + `orderBy()` on different
  fields, which Firestore can't run without a composite index. The
  comments in that file note this — if you add a _new_ query shaped like
  that, Firestore's own error message gives you a direct link to create
  the needed index.
- **Session timeout is 1 hour of inactivity** (`sessionManager.js`),
  tracked via mouse/keyboard/touch/scroll listeners, and force-signs the
  user out with a plain `alert()` + redirect.
- **Everything is UNIMED-specific copy** (course codes, university name)
  hardcoded directly into `index.html` meta tags and `landing.js` — if
  this is ever repurposed for another school, that's manual find/replace,
  not a config flag.

---

_This document reflects the codebase after the round of fixes covering:
the login white-screen, the recurring PWA install prompt, the Friend
Groups "undefined quizzes" bug, and the marketplace payment-confirmation
screen._
